module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.silent_first_wins_rebind_of_a_generic_type_variable_in_unify_generics

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread, CeilingAmbiguous }

data silent_first_wins_rebind_of_a_generic_type_variable_in_unify_generics: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "silent_first_wins_rebind_of_a_generic_type_variable_in_unify_generics" as NonEmptyStr,
  rung: RungUnread { cause: CeilingAmbiguous },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: unify_generics folds a call's actuals onto one accumulator and its already-bound arm is `if map_has(acc.subst, g) { acc }` — when a parameter already carries a binding, the later actual is DISCARDED and nothing is said, so a call whose arguments instantiate the same formal type variable to two different types is ACCEPTED on the first binding alone. The caller then receives a declared return type instantiated with a binding that no argument of the call implies. This is a fail-open: the compiler knows the two actuals disagree, prints nothing, and keeps typing the call as if the first one were the whole story (DESIGN section 5, an error must not masquerade as success).",

    "THE SAME SHAPE HID THE DEFECT THAT MADE IT MEASURABLE. The producer repair in the #14152 lane (src/v1/04_infer.dag call arm, commit 81824abfa3) is what first produced a corpus whose Map-returning generic calls disagree about K and V: before it, the Map-family rebinds compared a call's type against the callee's OWN formal parameters, because an argument-less generic call left the instantiation map empty and `substitute_generics_apply` short-circuits on an empty map, handing the declaration's own K/V to the caller as if instantiated. HOW MANY is not quoted: the census that counted them is a lane instrument that is not part of this change, and DESIGN section 6 makes a number citable only by the producer that re-derives it. Those rows were invisible to this class precisely because the fold was silent: one silent defect was masking another.",

    "MEASUREMENT, AND WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT QUOTED HERE. Reporting the conflict turns main's silence into hard type mismatches across the emitted closures where main produces none, and those mismatches are of three kinds, defined mechanically by what the diagnostic shows a reader: (1) GENUINE DISAGREEMENT -- the bound side and the later actual are different types, which is this row's class; (2) the binding being rebound renders as a KNOWN FABRICATED PLACEHOLDER, `Product(Unit<>)` or an empty `Primitive()`, which blames a call site for a slot that was never a type anyone wrote and belongs to `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.error_type_fabricated_as_a_type_by_total_accessors`; (3) IDENTICAL RENDERING -- expected and got print the same string, so the reader sees 'expected X, got X' and cannot tell a deliberate refusal from a broken comparator. NO COUNTS ARE QUOTED, for the reason DESIGN section 6 gives: a number is citable only by a producer that re-derives it, and the census that classified these rows is a lane instrument that is not part of this change, so transcribing its tallies here would leave numbers that rot unreachably. Anyone needing the split re-derives it by running the two-closure comparison over the head that carries the conflict report; the three definitions above are what makes that run reproducible rather than a re-typing of a table.",

    "CONSEQUENCE FOR A BUILD, FROM CODE RATHER THAN FROM A LOG. The conflict reuses the shared `type_mismatch_error`, which is SeverityError; `is_interpreter_blocking_diagnostic` (src/v1/stage0/src/v1_std_core.rs) maps TypeMismatch to Blocking, and the compile-clean gate (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs, with `compile_clean_diagnostic_is_hard` in cli_run/compile_clean.rs) refuses to emit while a blocking diagnostic stands. So a corpus containing such a call cannot be built while the conflict is reported -- and the first repair is NOT to soften the report: main's green build is green BECAUSE of the fail-open, so any fix must remove the disagreement rather than its visibility. The per-run counts (how many modules, how many diagnostics, whether emission was refused) are observable on any CI run of the head that carries the report and are deliberately not transcribed here; PR-2's acceptance gate is the entry point that re-derives them.",

    "REPAIR STATUS, stated against the commit that carries this row rather than against the lane. NOT YET ON MAIN: the report itself -- `UnifyGenericsAcc { subst, diagnostics }`, which turns the already-bound arm into a decision and emits the conflict-path diagnostic, with the RED `generic_type_variable_rebind_conflict_is_reported_when_a_bound_variable_gets_a_different_type` and an arm-naming mutation control. It is deliberately NOT in this change: reporting the conflict makes the corpus refuse emission (measured at the time of writing: hard type mismatches appear in both emitted closures where main has none, and emission is refused on the head while main emits its seed modules; the counts are re-derivable by PR-2's gate and are not transcribed here), so a PR carrying both the report and the producer repair can only be green by softening the report. What THIS change carries is NOT a repair of the producer, and it carries NO guard either. A boundary guard over this invariant was built, measured, and then DELETED rather than shipped, because its forbidden state is expressible nowhere it runs. Probe evidence, not assertion: four candidate constructs were compiled and walked for minted type variables -- an unannotated generic record literal (which should reach the name-keyed mint `map_insert(acc, g, type_variable_node(id: g))` in `infer_record_lit_structural` (src/v1/04_infer.dag)), an annotated one, a builtin empty-set element, and the argument-less generic call the guard pinned -- and ALL FOUR reported zero type-variable positions reachable from a compiled function body on main. The probe is a one-off, not an instrument, so it is named here as a reasoning step and its numbers are not transcribed; what makes the claim checkable is the code, cited next, not the run. So the assertion that no variable was minted was satisfied on main by there being nothing for it to satisfy: DESIGN 4b, a check whose forbidden state is expressible nowhere it runs is a decoration, green by construction and later cited as coverage. It also cannot be given a positive control at this rung: the substitution map the mint would have to write, call_subst, is local to infer_call_arguments_generic_pass (its parameter call_subst: Map<String, Node>, src/v1/04_infer.dag) and is not observable from a v1 unit test, so the only way to make such a guard go red is to hand-build a node tree and thereby test the test's own walk rather than the compiler. It is therefore recorded here as a MISSING NEXT-RUNG CARRIER, not as coverage: what is needed first is a claim-DSL helper that reports the type-variable ids the compiler minted for a call, together with its own RED on a tree that does mint. The helpers the claim DSL exposes today report emit equality, diagnostic census counts, call edges and leaf guards -- never type-variable identity -- so that helper is a new carrier with its own RED, not a line in this change. The mint itself (fresh per-call-site ids at the ExprCall call arm, narrowed to argument-less calls) WAS built and measured, and is deliberately NOT shipped: the emitter cannot carry a minted variable in any id form, so the producer defect this row describes remains OPEN on main, where an argument-less generic call still hands the callee's own formal parameters to the caller as if instantiated. Read this row as documentation of an open class with its blockers named, not as a closed defect and not as a pinned boundary. RUNG OF THIS ROW, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-CLAIMED: this row is a .dag document, so what executes over it is the corpus parser and the readers -- not a check that can go red on a regression. Its parse is proven rather than assumed: the corpus parse the heal step runs (gunbc run over --source-root dag and src/v2 with --entry dag/gunbc/heal_candidate_stage0.dag --function heal_stage0_overlap_refusal_wet) is the authority, and an earlier revision of this row used raw double quotes inside its own string fields, which a heuristic checker called clean while that parse refused the corpus. The heuristic is not cited here as evidence of anything. The producer repair is a separate change blocked on the emitter item above, and the conflict report follows as its own change once the corpus clears under it; the conflict-path diagnostic named above is that change's RED, and the EmissionRefused it produces at a seed module is its gate. NOT PLANNED AS PART OF EITHER: the refine-don't-fix rule (an uninformative binding -- a TypeVariable, or a Product(Unit<>) placeholder -- REFINED by an informative one instead of fixing the slot). The census described above satisfies the pre-registered predicate for building it, but the two upstream classes named above (fabricated placeholders, identical renderings) are its prerequisites, and it must be measured against a baseline that can already see the conflict -- so after the report, not beside it.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: the type-unification authority (a total fold that drops information and reports nothing). CEILING: the fold's already-bound arm becomes a decision — refine, refuse, or keep — and each choice needs its own control, so the ceiling is reached once the report exists and is not exceeded by softening it. NOT SCHEDULED, AS A BOUNDED OPERATOR DECISION: the disposition of the fabricated-placeholder rows and the identical-rendering rows; the decision is scoped to this lane. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: the refine-don't-fix rule shared by unify_generics and unify_lambda_solves, admitted once the report is on main, so that a rule change can be measured against a baseline that can already see the conflict.",

    "FRAGILITY RECORDED, NOT FIXED (1), and the boundary where it belongs: `unify_generics` records NO binding when an actual's relevant leaf is the CALLER'S OWN type variable. Measured consequence, on a real corpus call: `fn witness_measured_rollup_differs_from_predicted_zero<S>` (dag/std/realization_measurement.dag) passes a `Measure<Time, S, Nat>` actual whose leaf is the caller's own `S`; no binding is recorded, so a caller-side test of 'is this parameter already instantiated' is false for a parameter the actuals should have settled. Treating that absence as 'uninstantiable' and minting a fresh variable is a FABRICATION -- it replaces a variable the caller named with one no actual mentions, which is a silent wrong binding, the class this row is about. The mint that was built therefore applied only to a call carrying no argument nodes at all; a call that passes actuals keeps main's behaviour. THAT BOUNDARY IS CURRENTLY UNPINNED, and is stated here as a requirement rather than as coverage: the executable row that pinned it was deleted as a decoration for the reason given in the REPAIR STATUS receipt, so nothing today fails if a future mint is widened across it. Anyone unblocking the mint must land that carrier first, together with its RED, and must not treat the sentence in this receipt as a check. The repair of the absence itself lives in `unify_generics` (an uninformative binding should be refined, not fixed, and that rule is shared with `unify_lambda_solves`), not here, and it is routed as Charter 1's question -- not fixed by this row's author.",

    "FRAGILITY RECORDED, NOT FIXED (2), AND IT IS THE BLOCKER FOR THE DEFERRED MINT: the emitter derives a type variable's IDENTITY from its NAME at three sites, so any variable that is not under a name the compiler can recompute loses rendering, its Clone bound, and its clone-bound pre-pass -- silently. Site 1, rendering: the type-argument arm of the type-argument renderer in src/v1/05_emit.dag, renders a type argument only when `tn != \"\" && tn == var_id` -- the written name must equal the variable's id -- so a minted id that is not an authored name is not emitted as a generic argument at all, and the position collapses to whatever the expression defaulted to. Site 2, the fold-accumulator bridge: src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag emits `HashMap::new() /* BRIDGE: fold empty_map accumulator type unresolved */` when a fold accumulator's map type cannot be resolved, so an unbound map variable is substituted by a placeholder comment rather than reported. Site 3, the Clone bound: `emit_item_header_clone_param_names` and `v1_item_wf_propagated_clone_bounded_param_names` (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag) return declared parameter NAMES, and `emit_type_params_with_clone_bound` (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag) appends `: Clone` by comparing each param's name against them -- `if pname == clone_param { concat(pascal, \": Clone\") } else { pascal }` -- so a variable that is not under a declared name gets no bound and nothing is reported. Measured: the SAME emitted function carries the bound under a name-keyed mint and loses it under a call-site-keyed mint (`pub fn witness_measured_rollup_differs_from_predicted_zero<S: Clone>` against `<S>`), the compiler stays mute, and it surfaces only when cargo builds the emitted product, as `error[E0277]: the trait bound <name>: Clone is not satisfied`; with the rendering site blocked instead, the same class surfaces as `error: UNRESOLVED_CompilerError` in a generated `HashMap::<compile_error!(\"UNRESOLVED_CompilerError\"), compile_error!(\"UNRESOLVED_CompilerError\")>::new()` for a `fold(init: empty_map(), ...)` accumulator the `fold(init: empty_map(), ...)` accumulator in src/v2/compiler/symbol_index_fill.dag. Both were found only by `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:self-host` on the emitted product, which is why it is now in the pre-push gate. DISPOSITION: the mint is its own change, BLOCKED on the emitter keying rendering and clone bounds on a stable variable identity rather than a name -- three sites, one property. Charter 6 owns that item. Until it lands, no per-call-site mint can be shipped in any id form, and this row's producer defect stays open. ",
    "V2 ANALOG AT THE MATCH-BINDING SEAM, STATED WITHOUT OVERSTATING IT (folded 2026-10-20 from a proposed row whose class identity duplicated this one). `v2.compiler.infer` `infer_match_generic_formal` has the same already-bound shape as this row's fold: when the binder already carries an instance, the arm returns the instances UNCHANGED and the later produced type is dropped. WHAT THAT DOES NOT ESTABLISH IS SILENCE: this row's v1 fold is silent because nothing consumes the dropped actual, while the v2 helper's consumers do judge it -- `infer_judge_generic_formal` instantiates the formal and hands it to `infer_judge_declared_position`, `infer_judge_application_argument_instantiating` judges an already-bound bare type variable's actual against the STORED instance, and `infer_judge_formal_args` carries the instance list across argument pairs. So the v2 shape is 'first binding retained, every later actual judged against that binding', which is a different claim from this row's 'later actual discarded and nothing said'. The two must not be merged into one class label, and the v2 helper's first-wins alone is not evidence of an unjudged actual. Recorded here because the same-fold-shape is what a future reader will otherwise conflate; the judgment difference is what keeps the classes separate.",

  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.infer", decl_name: "unify_generics", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.compiler.infer", decl_name: "infer_match_generic_formal", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
